first_design.rule:
One value, one place. A value is made once, in the place that fits everything the program will ever do with it, and it stays there for as long as anything points at it.

The program's words say what kind of value it is: held alone, `owns`, a class object, or shared with `shares`, `shares mut` or `thread_shared`. The compiler decides only two things: when the value's block is made, and which header the block has (8, 12 or 32 bytes, read-only or writable for a static). It decides them from every line that uses the value, following the value through hand-overs, calls, returns, fields and lists, the way it already follows views and stored functions. It never adds a block that no word or type asks for.

So a line that shares a value, makes a link or an owner of it, or gives its address to C finds the value already where it needs to be. No view, link or C pointer ever sees the value move or its header change.

Only four kinds of line move a value, and each one first ends whatever pointed at the old place:
- a hand-over, which ends the giver's views (BCK-4);
- a list growing, which ends views into the elements it stores inline (verdict 81);
- verdict 114's hand-over to another thread, which leaves the old block marked ended (and stops the program if C keeps the object's address);
- a reload.

It follows that every sharing word may follow a local of the function in the same way: `thread_shared s` after `s = Settings()` works just as `shares mut inv` does.

