method1_repair.rule:
One value, one place, and the holder's type says which place.

A value lives in one place for its whole life, and every holder's word says what kind of place that is:
- held alone: inline in its owner, or a class object's block with a one-thread header;
- shared on one thread (`shares`, `shares mut`): a block with a one-thread header;
- shared between threads (`thread_shared`): a block that keeps the one-thread header and puts its two extra counting words after the value, so its fields sit where a one-thread value's do.

So the code for any holder (its reads, its counts, its end) follows from its type alone, in every function and every package.

A sharing line changes what its name is. From `shares x`, `shares mut x` or `thread_shared x`, the local `x` is one of the owners, of the type the word gives. It stays so after any point where a path through the line meets a path around it. Handing `x` over hands over that owner, so a place that holds the value alone does not take it.

The function that shares a value gives it that place from where the value's storage begins in that function:
- a struct goes in its block;
- a class object the function makes gets the header the line needs;
- a class object that reaches the function from elsewhere and is made `thread_shared` is moved into its block where it reaches the function, by verdict 114's step (or at the sharing line, where nothing could tell the difference).

So a view, a raw address or a C address taken before the sharing line still points at the value after it. Nothing is decided across a package's edge or from the whole program.

Every link to a value shared between threads is made through a `thread_shared` name. A one-thread link to it is refused wherever the compiler sees it made. A link made before the value reached the function stays on the old block, which then says gone (verdict 114).

Only four kinds of line move a value, and each one first ends whatever pointed at the old place:
- a hand-over of a value held alone;
- a reshape of a holder that stores the value inline;
- a move of a class object that a link on this thread points at, either to another thread or into a block shared between threads (verdict 114);
- a reload.

A static is placed by its type. A class object or a `thread_shared` value in it, or in anything it owns, sits in writable memory and is built when compiling. Its links count on its module's home thread, and a link made of it only reads it.

It follows that every sharing word may follow a local of the function in the same way: `thread_shared s` after `s = Settings()` works just as `shares mut inv` does.

